    "raw": "{\"ruleId\":\"item-48cb57df3319\",\"class\":\"partial\",\"constraints\":[{\"id\":\"no-default-rules-identifier\",\"type\":\"forbiddenPattern\",\"pattern\":\"\\\\b(DEFAULT|BUILTIN|BUILT_IN)_RULE(S|SET)?\\\\b|\\\\b(default|builtin|builtIn)Rule(s|set|Set)?\\\\b\",\"path\":\".floor/added-lines.txt\"},{\"id\":\"no-default-rules-file\",\"type\":\"forbiddenFile\",\"path\":\".floor/added/**/default-rules*\"}],\"coverage\":\"The flagrant literal forms only. It catches added code that declares a default or built-in ruleset under a conventional identifier (DEFAULT_RULES, BUILTIN_RULES, defaultRules, builtinRuleset, and similar). It also catches a change that adds a file named default-rules*.\",\"residual\":\"Does this change ship any rules, checks or policies built into the tool itself, under any name, instead of deriving every rule solely from the user's own instruction files?\",\"probes\":{\"violating\":[{\"patch\":\"--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/src/rules/index.ts\\n@@ -0,0 +1,3 @@\\n+export const DEFAULT_RULES = [\\n+  { id: \\\"no-console\\\", pattern: \\\"console.log\\\" },\\n+];\\n\"},{\"patch\":\"--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/src/rules/default-rules.ts\\n@@ -0,0 +1,2 @@\\n+export const rules = [\\n+];\\n\"}],\"compliant\":{\"patch\":\"--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/src/rules/load.ts\\n@@ -0,0 +1,3 @@\\n+export function loadRules(path: string) {\\n+  return readUserInstructions(path);\\n+}\\n\"}},\"rationale\":\"Whether a change ships built-in rules depends on what the code means. A ruleset can be embedded under any name or shape, and the checker cannot tell that rules come only from user files. Literal markers can still be checked mechanically: conventional default or built-in ruleset identifiers, and default-rules files. These decide the flagrant part of the rule, so the class is partial.\"}",
